<section class="section" id="challenge-response" aria-labelledby="challenge-response-heading">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Challenge–Response Record</p>
    <h2 id="challenge-response-heading">Documented pressures, action and measures of progress</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Statements from the 2022 MEE remain date-bound. Later notifications and sanctions are shown separately
      where they address an earlier recommendation, but no completion is inferred without outcome evidence.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="pressure-grid">
    <article class="pressure-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Connectivity</span>
      <h3>Linear reserve form and landscape links</h3>
      <p><strong>Evidence:</strong> The 2022 MEE identified the reserve’s linear form as an inherent vulnerability, while also recording connectivity potential towards Ranthambore–Ramgarh Vishdhari and Gandhi Sagar through Bhainsrodgarh.</p>
      <p><strong>Documented response:</strong> The 5 October 2023 notification inserted Bhainsrodgarh-area entries into the Critical Tiger Habitat schedule and revised the reserve total. The Rajasthan LTAP also presents a 30-year connectivity vision.</p>
      <p class="pressure-status"><strong>Status:</strong> <span class="evidence-status">Notified + long-term planning</span></p>
      <p><strong>Public measures:</strong> Periodic landscape-scale occupancy summaries, habitat-condition trends and legally secured corridor area, without publishing animal locations.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="pressure-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Infrastructure</span>
      <h3>Road and rail mitigation remains a continuing requirement</h3>
      <p><strong>Evidence:</strong> The MEE reported wall fencing on some railway mortality-prone segments and an underground tunnel under construction for the eight-lane highway. The same evaluation continued to identify unmitigated exposure along road and rail sections.</p>
      <p><strong>Documented response:</strong> The Bharatmala wildlife-clearance record contains mitigation commitments, and the MEE recorded work underway in September 2022.</p>
      <p class="pressure-status"><strong>Status:</strong> <span class="evidence-status">Partial / last site-specific evidence 2022</span></p>
      <p><strong>Public measures:</strong> Structure-completion status, independent usage monitoring, maintenance records and aggregated wildlife-mortality trends.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="pressure-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Habitat</span>
      <h3>Invasive plants and grassland condition</h3>
      <p><strong>Evidence:</strong> The MEE identified <em>Prosopis</em>, <em>Lantana</em> and <em>Ocimum canum</em> infestation as limiting factors for herbivores. The later CAG audit reported the absence of an adequate long-term invasive-species policy at the wider State level during its audit period.</p>
      <p><strong>Documented response:</strong> The FY2025–26 APO sanctions habitat improvement and weed-eradication work for MHTR.</p>
      <p class="pressure-status"><strong>Status:</strong> <span class="evidence-status">Sanctioned, completion not inferred</span></p>
      <p><strong>Public measures:</strong> Treated hectares, method, repeat-treatment need, native vegetation recovery and before-after satellite or field evidence.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="pressure-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Prey and recovery</span>
      <h3>Prey augmentation requires measured ecological outcomes</h3>
      <p><strong>Evidence:</strong> The 2022 MEE recommended in-situ conservation breeding and intensive monitoring of released prey populations, including demography and survival.</p>
      <p><strong>Documented response:</strong> Prey augmentation and associated habitat work appear among the approved MHTR priorities in the FY2025–26 APO.</p>
      <p class="pressure-status"><strong>Status:</strong> <span class="evidence-status">Recommended + sanctioned</span></p>
      <p><strong>Public measures:</strong> Pre-release health protocols, aggregate release and survival results, prey-density trends and independent review. Exact release sites should remain unpublished.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="pressure-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Coexistence</span>
      <h3>Villages, livelihoods, livestock and conflict response</h3>
      <p><strong>Evidence:</strong> The MEE recorded varied relocation stages in September 2022 and noted future human–tiger interface risk. The CAG audit also identified slow relocation and community-participation weaknesses across Rajasthan tiger reserves during its historical audit period.</p>
      <p><strong>Documented response:</strong> The FY2025–26 APO includes conflict compensation support, livestock and dog vaccination, local wildlife volunteers and eco-development activities. Rajasthan revised human death and injury ex-gratia amounts in April 2026.</p>
      <p class="pressure-status"><strong>Status:</strong> <span class="evidence-status">Active policy + sanctioned work</span></p>
      <p><strong>Public measures:</strong> Voluntary-consent records, household rehabilitation outcomes, grievance resolution, payment time, vaccination coverage and community representation.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="pressure-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Capacity and transparency</span>
      <h3>Protection strengths coexist with staffing and reporting gaps</h3>
      <p><strong>Evidence:</strong> The MEE described operational M-STrIPES, surveillance and Border Home Guard deployment as strengths, while reporting substantial frontline vacancies, limited formal wildlife training, sparse research and minimal public information in 2022.</p>
      <p><strong>Documented response:</strong> The FY2025–26 APO approves protection, communications, Home Guard/STPF support, monitoring and maintenance components.</p>
      <p class="pressure-status"><strong>Status:</strong> <span class="evidence-status">Sanctioned, current staffing not verified</span></p>
      <p><strong>Public measures:</strong> Aggregate sanctioned-versus-filled posts, training days, annual research summaries, utilization certificates and non-sensitive management outcomes.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section split-panel mee-evidence-panel" aria-labelledby="mee-heading">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Management Effectiveness Evaluation</p>
    <h2 id="mee-heading">What the September 2022 MEE established</h2>
    <p>
      The fifth-cycle evaluation rated MHTR “Good” at 60.16%, improving from “Fair” in the 2014 and
      2018 cycles. The assessment documented landscape connectivity, unified core-buffer administration,
      protection deployment, vegetation recovery inside the enclosure, partial linear-infrastructure
      mitigation and progress in voluntary relocation.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also listed invasive plants, feral cattle, infrastructure exposure, frontline vacancies, weak
      participation by Eco-Development Committees, limited public information, insufficient research and
      delays in fund use. These are findings from a September 2022 visit, not a description of every condition in 2026.
    </p>
  </div>
